<description>
Draw the green perimeter of the parallelogram whose opposite corners are the two marker pixels.

Input structure
- The input is a same-size canvas with a dominant background and exactly two foreground marker pixels of one color.
- The marker-to-marker offset has both row and column components, with unequal magnitudes.

Rule
- Decompose the vector between markers into a 45-degree diagonal vector plus the remaining horizontal/vertical vector.
- Treat those two vectors as adjacent sides of a parallelogram, with the markers as opposite corners.
- Paint the four sides green while preserving the marker pixels.

Key insight
- The outline is not a direct line interpolation; it is the perimeter of the parallelogram induced by the diagonal and axis-aligned parts of the marker offset.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "two same-color marker pixels on the background, used as opposite corners of an implied parallelogram",
  "transform": "decompose the marker-to-marker offset into a 45-degree diagonal side and the remaining horizontal/vertical side; paint green on the parallelogram perimeter and keep the markers unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 11-13 rows x 13-14 cols; background BLACK
  markers:
    count: exactly 2
    color: AZURE (8) [observed]; any single non-bg color works
    placement: different row AND different column; rest of grid is background
  offset:
    abs_dr: 2-11; abs_dc: 6-10; abs_dr != abs_dc (required by solver)
    diag_len (min of abs_dr, abs_dc): 2-7
    straight_len (|abs_dr - abs_dc|): 2-8
    both >= 1 required; manhattan distance 12-18 [observed]
  parallelogram:
    decompose marker-to-marker vector into 45-degree diagonal + axis-aligned remainder
    four corners: p, p+straight_vec, p+diag_vec, q (where q = p+straight_vec+diag_vec)
    all four sides drawn in GREEN (3); markers preserved as AZURE
    green_cell_count = 2*(diag_len + straight_len) - 2
    all four corners and all perimeter cells must be in-bounds
    interior is hollow (stays background)

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct (constraints are simple enough)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H in [11,15], W in [13,16]
    2. Pick marker p=(r1,c1) randomly within grid
    3. Pick offset (dr, dc) with abs_dr in [2, H-2], abs_dc in [2, W-2], abs_dr != abs_dc, both > 0
       signs chosen randomly from {-1, +1}
    4. Compute q = p + (dr, dc) and all 4 parallelogram corners
    5. Verify all perimeter cells are in-bounds; reject if not
    6. Place two AZURE markers on otherwise-BLACK grid
```
